= Conclusion & outlook

ComplAI addresses a clear market need by helping European FinTech companies reduce the time, costs, and complexity of regulatory compliance against the many European frameworks. Its AI-powered "pre-lawyer" approach supports regulatory assessment, continuous monitoring, and document generation while keeping qualified legal professionals involved in final validation.

The business opportunity is substantial: ComplAI targets a €42 billion TAM, a €110 million SAM, and an initial €1.65 million SOM in Germany and Italy. Revenue will come from an accessible SaaS copilot, higher-value regulatory monitoring subscriptions, and a pay-per-use document drafting service.

The next stage will be focusing on converting the current concept into a validated minimum viable product. Priority actions include developing the regulatory assessment and retrieval system from official sources, testing its outputs with qualified legal professionals, and running pilot programmes with a small number of FinTech startups. Progress should be evaluated through outcome-based indicators such as the Issue Remediation Rate, the Legal Verification Acceptance Rate, and the First-Time Submission Pass Rate (see the Appendix for the full KPI definitions and targets).

In the longer term, ComplAI will be able to move beyond FinTech and adapt its regulatory infrastructure to other highly regulated sectors, such as insurance, healthcare, energy, telecommunications, and data-intensive digital services, as well as completely different kinds of business model.

Ultimately, ComplAI's ambition will not simply be to make compliance faster or cheaper, but to transform regulation from a barrier into an operational advantage. Through this later step, ComplAI can become the trusted bridge between innovation and regulation, enabling companies to build and scale with greater confidence, ultimately being part of the greater European development that is to come in the context of the many challenges of the current geopolitical instability.
